endobj

887 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 291 0 R/E(What never changes, and what it tells us about who we are.)/Pg 46 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

888 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 291 0 R/E(The sun is rising. The colours of the sky shift from one heavenly shade to another across the vast horizon. A gentle breeze stirs the leaves, their soft rustling blending into the quiet morning. Birds chirp all around; some are leaving their nests, while others soar freely through the open sky. Below, the pond reflects the changing colours of the sky, its mirror-like surface disturbed only by occasional ripples.)/Pg 46 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

889 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 291 0 R/E(Bare feet touch the dew-covered grass. A cool breeze passes through the body, bringing a gentle tingling. Freshness arises, followed by happiness without any apparent reason. A desire to remain there forever slowly takes hold, and with it the thought of building a small shack nearby, so that one could return every day and witness this beauty.)/Pg 46 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

890 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 291 0 R/E(A few minutes later, the road, silent)/Pg 46 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

891 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 291 0 R/T(Fundamental Knowledge)/E(Fundamental Knowledge)/Pg 46 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

892 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 291 0 R/E(Chaitanya)/Pg 46 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

893 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 291 0 R/E(now, is bustling with cars, jeeps, scooters, bicycles and pedestrians. The traffic slows, then crawls to a halt. Horns blare. Hawkers weave between vehicles. People try to cut across or squeeze through gaps where hardly any space exists. Then one vehicle touches another, just a minor scratch, and that is enough. Shouting begins, arguments escalate, and a crowd gathers to watch the drama unfold as if the morning has found a new form of entertainment.)/Pg 46 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

894 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 291 0 R/E(Stuck in the traffic, the body begins to sweat. Uneasiness creeps in, followed by frustration and simmering anger. The desire to return home grows stronger, even though there is nothing urgent to attend to.)/Pg 46 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

895 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 291 0 R/E(One scene brings freshness and happiness; the other brings discomfort and anger. The surroundings change, and so do the sensations, feelings and desires within us. Amid these changing experiences, a simple question arises:)/Pg 46 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

896 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 291 0 R/E<FEFF092E09410915094D0924093F092C094B0927002D002000330035>/Pg 46 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

897 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 292 0 R/E(Is there anything that does not change at all? Even one thing?)/Pg 47 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

898 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 292 0 R/E(If we stay with this question, careful observation leads to an acknowledgement: something unchanging is present.)/Pg 47 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

899 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 292 0 R/T(What Is the Unchanging Like?)/E(What Is the Unchanging Like?)/Pg 47 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

900 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 292 0 R/E(Let us look more closely.)/Pg 47 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

901 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 292 0 R/E(The unchanging cannot be grasped like an ordinary object. Shape, size, colour and boundaries help us identify and distinguish things, but they belong to the world of changing forms.)/Pg 47 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

902 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 292 0 R/E(It is unbound and indivisible. Division implies distinct parts, and a thing that changes cannot be entirely unchanging. Decay is change, and so are birth and death: whatever begins or ends undergoes a change of state. The unchanging is therefore beyond birth, decay and death.)/Pg 47 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

903 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 292 0 R/E(It cannot be located like an ordinary object, yet its presence cannot be denied. It is present through happiness and sadness, like an unattached friend, unaffected by whichever state passes through. The more one tries to grasp it as an object, the more elusive it becomes.)/Pg 47 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

904 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 292 0 R/E(Nothing can be added to it, for whatever can be added belongs to the changing. Nothing can be removed from it either, because nothing was ever held there in the first place.)/Pg 47 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

905 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 292 0 R/E(Is it one or many? Telling two things apart needs a boundary or a quality, )/Pg 47 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

906 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 292 0 R/E(and the unchanging has neither. By this reasoning, it is one.)/Pg 47 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

907 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 292 0 R/E(Pure. Indivisible. Unbound. Unperturbed\201. Unattached\201. Unique\201. Elusive, yet ever present.)/Pg 47 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

908 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 292 0 R/E(Since it has no attributes or features, no description can capture it. Contemplation therefore reaches the limit of language, and ends in silence.)/Pg 47 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

909 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 292 0 R/E<FEFF092E09410915094D0924093F092C094B0927002D002000330036>/Pg 47 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

910 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 292 0 R/T(The Changing and the Unchanging)/E(The Changing and the Unchanging)/Pg 47 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

911 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 292 0 R/E(The changing and the unchanging complement each other. Consider the changing colours of the sky. To recognise that the sky looks different now, we compare the present with what we observed before. The two experiences differ, yet we recognise the change because both are known within awareness.)/Pg 47 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

912 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 292 0 R/E(The unchanging witnesses the changing. Experiences appear, evolve and pass away against its background, and the changing is only a reflection of it. Seen closely, the two are not separate.)/Pg 47 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

913 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 292 0 R/E(There are not two. This is fundamental knowledge.)/Pg 47 0 R/K[16]>>
endobj

914 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 292 0 R/T(Who Is It?)/E(Who Is It?)/Pg 47 0 R/K[17]>>
endobj

915 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 292 0 R/E<>/Pg 47 0 R/K[18]>>
endobj

916 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 292 0 R/Pg 47 0 R/A[2165 0 R 0]/K[19]>>
endobj

917 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 293 0 R/Pg 48 0 R/A[2166 0 R 0]/K[0]>>
endobj

918 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 293 0 R/E(Let us explore this closer to home. What can we discover by examining our own experience?)/Pg 48 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

919 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 293 0 R/E(The sensations, feelings, emotions and desires of the sunrise and the traffic keep changing. None of them stays the same. Even change itself is impermanent: however vast or lasting a change seems, it too passes. Natural calamities are a blunt reminder.)/Pg 48 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

920 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 293 0 R/E(The body and mind also change. Consider an accident in which you fracture a limb and suffer abrasions and scratches. Amid the pain, what is the first reassurance you give yourself?)/Pg 48 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

921 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 293 0 R/E(It could have been worse. At least I am safe. Yes, I survived.)/Pg 48 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

922 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 293 0 R/E(Something in you says: I remain intact. The body has changed and the pain is intense, yet the \215I\216 continues through all of it.)/Pg 48 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

923 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 293 0 R/E(The body changes, but I remain. The contents of the mind change, but I remain. The flow of experience changes, but I remain.)/Pg 48 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

924 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 293 0 R/E(But what exactly is this "I"? Is it the body, the mind, the changing  )/Pg 48 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

925 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 293 0 R/E(experiences, or the one who witnesses them? Do not accept an answer merely because it has been stated. Observe.)/Pg 48 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

926 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 293 0 R/T(A Small Exercise)/E(A Small Exercise)/Pg 48 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

927 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 293 0 R/E(Read these sentences slowly and observe what changes in each one:)/Pg 48 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

928 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 293 0 R/E(I am trembling. I feel a tingling sensation. I am healthy. I am injured. I am happy. I am sad. I am a doctor. I am)/Pg 48 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

929 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 293 0 R/E(a father. I am a failure. I want to build a home.)/Pg 48 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

930 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 293 0 R/E(In "I am happy", "happy" is the predicate and "I" is the subject. In every sentence above, the predicate is different. Now look for yourself: as the words after "I" change, does the one saying "I" change with them, or does it remain the same?)/Pg 48 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

931 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 293 0 R/E(If, on looking, the one saying \215I\216 remains the same, the pattern is plain: objects change; the subject does not. As reasoned above, the unchanging is one, so this subject is that unchanging. Hence:)/Pg 48 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

932 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 293 0 R/E(The "I" is unchanging.)/Pg 48 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

933 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 293 0 R/E(The "I" is the experiencer.)/Pg 48 0 R/K[16]>>
endobj

934 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 293 0 R/E(The "I" is the seer.)/Pg 48 0 R/K[17]>>
endobj

935 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 293 0 R/E(Remembering this simple knowledge loosens the veils of identification and has the power to dissolve suffering.)/Pg 48 0 R/K[18]>>
endobj

936 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 293 0 R/E(Peace.)/Pg 48 0 R/K[19]>>
endobj

937 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 293 0 R/E<FEFF09650020095000200936094D09300940002009260924094D0924093E0924094D09300947092F093E092F00200928092E090300200965>/Pg 48 0 R/K[20]>>
endobj

938 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 293 0 R/E<FEFF092E09410915094D0924093F092C094B0927002D002000330037>/Pg 48 0 R/K[21]>>
endobj

939 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 293 0 R/E<>/Pg 48 0 R/K[22]>>
endobj

940 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 293 0 R/Pg 48 0 R/A[2167 0 R 0]/K[23]>>
endobj

941 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 293 0 R/Pg 48 0 R/A[2120 0 R 0]/K[24]>>
endobj

942 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 294 0 R/Pg 49 0 R/A[2168 0 R 0]/K[0]>>
endobj

943 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 294 0 R/E(The ultimate truth is that there is experience and an experiencer. There is nothing beyond these two, and even this apparent duality is an act of the mind. When they merge, experience and experiencer resolve into Experiencing\204a dynamic Presence.)/Pg 49 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

944 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 294 0 R/T(Why Am I So Certain?)/E(Why Am I So Certain?)/Pg 49 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

945 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 294 0 R/E(\215Ultimate truth\216 is a big claim. Surely, something so profound cannot be so simple. Can an ordinary, everyday experience reveal it?)/Pg 49 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

946 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 294 0 R/E(Yet the starting point is simple: we cannot deny that something is. Even the denial of existence is itself something that happens. Let us call this undeniable Presence.)/Pg 49 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

947 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 294 0 R/E(To understand its nature, we need only observe directly what is. Without analysing the particular contents of experience, we can recognise one simple fact: there is experience.)/Pg 49 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

948 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 294 0 R/E(The next question follows naturally: what knows this experience? What is )/Pg 49 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

949 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 294 0 R/T(The Ultimate Truth)/E(The Ultimate Truth)/Pg 49 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

950 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 294 0 R/T(Sadguru Shri Tarun Pradhaan)/E(Sadguru Shri Tarun Pradhaan)/Pg 49 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

951 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 294 0 R/E(experiencing it? We identify an experiencer, commonly called \215I,\216 the Self or consciousness. Thus, we arrive at two: experience and experiencer.)/Pg 49 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

952 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 294 0 R/E(Try denying experience. The act of denial itself is an experience. Now try denying the experiencer. Who or what is making that denial? Once again, the question points towards an experiencer. The very attempt to deny these two seems to reaffirm them.)/Pg 49 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

953 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 294 0 R/E(This is direct observation, requiring no prior theory or assumption. It is the certainty of this observation that leads us to call it truth. But why call it the ultimate truth? Could there be something beyond experience and experiencer?)/Pg 49 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

954 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 294 0 R/E(Let us investigate. Suppose something called \215X\216 underlies all experience. How would we know X? We would have to experience it. The moment we do, it becomes an experience, whatever its nature.)/Pg 49 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

955 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 294 0 R/E<FEFF092E09410915094D0924093F092C094B0927002D002000330038>/Pg 49 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

956 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 295 0 R/Pg 50 0 R/A[2169 0 R 0]/K[0]>>
endobj

957 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 295 0 R/E(Similarly, suppose something called \215Y\216 exists beyond the experiencer. We must ask: who experiences Y? If Y itself becomes the experiencer, the original experiencer becomes something experienced. Yet we are still left with experience and an experiencer.)/Pg 50 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

958 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 295 0 R/E(Thus, no matter how we approach the question, we arrive at these two. This is the basis of the claim that experience and experiencer constitute the Ultimate Truth: anything proposed beyond them must, to be known, appear as experience or as that which experiences.)/Pg 50 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

959 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 295 0 R/T(Experience, Experiencer and the Mind)/E(Experience, Experiencer and the Mind)/Pg 50 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

960 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 295 0 R/E(But a further question arises: what knows that there are two?)/Pg 50 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

961 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 295 0 R/E(The experience and the experiencer are engaged in experiencing. How do we arrive at the knowledge that they are two? The answer appears to be a third element: the mind, which recognises and distinguishes them.)/Pg 50 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

962 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 295 0 R/E(However, the mind itself can be )/Pg 50 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

963 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 295 0 R/E(experienced. Therefore, it belongs to the field of experience rather than being an independent experiencer. It is not a separate third entity; it is part of what is experienced.)/Pg 50 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

964 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 295 0 R/E(The mind acts as an intermediary, organising experience and presenting knowledge of experience and experiencer. What we call knowledge is organised experience, retained as structured memory. This knowledge, too, is experienced.)/Pg 50 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

965 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 295 0 R/E(The mind does not stand outside experience. It organises and separates what appears to be experience and experiencer, allowing the distinction between them to be recognised.)/Pg 50 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

966 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 295 0 R/T(There Are No Two)/E(There Are No Two)/Pg 50 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

967 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 295 0 R/E(If the mind itself is an experience, what remains when we see it as such?)/Pg 50 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

968 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 295 0 R/E(What remains is Presence: the happening called Experiencing. It is difficult to describe in words. It may be called suchness, or simply Presence\204intensely conscious, empty and eternally flowing. It is a changing stillness, beyond easy description.)/Pg 50 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

969 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 295 0 R/E(How does this happen? The mind must cease its habitual activity of dividing what is into experience and experiencer. It shifts attention between the two, creating the appearance of separation, with the mind seemingly standing between them.)/Pg 50 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

970 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 295 0 R/E(When this dividing activity ceases through will or intention, only Experiencing remains.)/Pg 50 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

971 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 295 0 R/E(The mind may still be present, but  )/Pg 50 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

972 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 295 0 R/E<FEFF092E09410915094D0924093F092C094B0927002D002000330039>/Pg 50 0 R/K[16]>>
endobj

973 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 296 0 R/Pg 51 0 R/A[2170 0 R 0]/K[0]>>
endobj

974 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 296 0 R/E(Through logical deduction. Observe that )/Pg 51 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

975 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 296 0 R/E(experience and experiencer always appear together: there can be no experience without an experiencer, and no experiencer without experience. This suggests that they may be one reality appearing as two through the activity of the mind.)/Pg 51 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

976 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 296 0 R/E(The mind both divides and unites. It cannot directly know oneness, yet it can conclude that there is nothing apart from it. The seeker encounters a paradox: what the mind distinguishes, it can also recognise as not truly separate. The term Advaita\204not-two\204is an attempt to express this most accurately.)/Pg 51 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

977 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 296 0 R/E(Foundation of Knowledge)/Pg 51 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

978 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 296 0 R/E(This inquiry is not merely an intellectual answer to the question of ultimate truth. It concerns the foundation of all knowledge.)/Pg 51 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

979 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 296 0 R/E(If experience and experiencer are the two fundamental aspects, understanding their nature becomes central to understanding what can be known. The  )/Pg 51 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

980 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 296 0 R/E(experience is no longer separated from the experiencer. The distinction between the two dissolves. Yet this raises another question: if the two are no longer distinguished, how can we know this state of oneness?)/Pg 51 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

981 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 296 0 R/E(The mind, as the holder and organiser of knowledge, cannot fully describe oneness. The most it can say is that there are no two. This is the essence of non-dualism, or Advaita.)/Pg 51 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

982 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 296 0 R/E(Oneness)/Pg 51 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

983 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 296 0 R/E(Does this mean the Ultimate Truth becomes false? Are there not two, but only one?)/Pg 51 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

984 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 296 0 R/E(The mind cannot directly know oneness; it can only arrive at the conclusion of oneness. If we say that oneness is being experienced, we imply an experiencer. Oneness is then divided into the experience of oneness and the one experiencing it.)/Pg 51 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

985 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 296 0 R/E(Oneness, therefore, lies beyond what the mind can claim to know. The most honest response may be: \215I do not know.\216 Or, more accurately, silence. There is a knowing that cannot be turned into ordinary knowledge.)/Pg 51 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

986 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 296 0 R/E(Strictly speaking, the distinction between experience and experiencer depends on the mind that recognises it. Without this mental distinction, there is no knowledge of two\204only Experiencing, undivided wholeness.)/Pg 51 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

987 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 296 0 R/E(How, then, does the mind arrive at the conclusion of oneness if it cannot know it directly?)/Pg 51 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

988 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 296 0 R/E<FEFF092E09410915094D0924093F092C094B0927002D002000340030>/Pg 51 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

989 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 297 0 R/E<FEFF092E09410915094D0924093F092C094B0927002D002000340031>/Pg 52 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

990 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 297 0 R/E(method is direct observation: observe, question and understand.)/Pg 52 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

991 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 297 0 R/E(How much can be known? The inquiry has no apparent end, but its essence may become clear quickly. The experiencer is directly accessible here and now. )/Pg 52 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

992 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 297 0 R/E(Experience is far more varied, perhaps seemingly infinite, yet many of its essential features can also be examined through immediate observation.)/Pg 52 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

993 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 297 0 R/E(As the inquiry deepens, the mind itself becomes an astonishing field of investigation\204a vast collection of structures and patterns. The individual mind may be understood as part of a Universal Mind, with the physical universe appearing as one among countless possible expressions of this greater field. )/Pg 52 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

994 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 297 0 R/E(Exploring every detail could become endless. The seeker, therefore, focuses on the essence and investigates details only when necessary.)/Pg 52 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

995 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 297 0 R/E(This is how the path of knowledge begins: with the recognition of oneness, or not-two. At the end of the journey, we return to the same ground, now with a deeper understanding.)/Pg 52 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

996 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 297 0 R/E(The purpose is not to accumulate endless information, but to investigate what is fundamental. The journey begins at home and, through inquiry, brings us home again\204towards the recognition of what has always been present.)/Pg 52 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

997 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 297 0 R/T(The Eye That Cannot See Itself)/E(The Eye That Cannot See Itself)/Pg 52 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

998 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 297 0 R/E(A student asked his teacher, \215I can see the world around me, but how can I see the one who is seeing?\216)/Pg 52 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

999 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 297 0 R/E(The teacher held up a mirror. \215What do you see?\216)/Pg 52 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

1000 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 297 0 R/E(\215My face,\216 said the student.)/Pg 52 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

1001 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 297 0 R/E(\215And who is looking at it?\216)/Pg 52 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

1002 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 297 0 R/E(\215I am.\216)/Pg 52 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

1003 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 297 0 R/E(\215Can you see the one who is looking, in the same way you see your face?\216)/Pg 52 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

1004 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 297 0 R/E(The student paused. \215No. I can see my reflection, but that is also something I observe.\216)/Pg 52 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

1005 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 297 0 R/E(The teacher nodded. \215Then keep enquiring. Do not turn the reflection into the observer, or an unanswered question into a ready-made belief.\216)/Pg 52 0 R/K[16]>>
endobj

1006 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 297 0 R/E(For the first time, the student stopped searching for an image of himself and simply noticed the act of seeing.)/Pg 52 0 R/K[17]>>
endobj

1007 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 297 0 R/E(The student asked, \215Then, will I ever find the answer?\216)/Pg 52 0 R/K[18]>>
endobj

1008 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 297 0 R/E(The teacher replied, \215Do not hurry to find an image of the one who sees. First, understand what you are searching for\204and whether it can ever be found as an object of experience.\216)/Pg 52 0 R/K[19]>>
endobj

1009 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 297 0 R/E(Reflection: The one who sees may not be found as another object to be seen. The enquiry begins by turning attention towards the one who asks.)/Pg 52 0 R/K[20]>>
endobj

1010 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 297 0 R/Pg 52 0 R/A[2171 0 R 0]/K[21]>>
endobj

1011 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 297 0 R/Pg 52 0 R/A[2120 0 R 0]/K[22]>>
endobj

1012 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 298 0 R/E(Not a verdict about God, but the place where knowing, having dropped everything false, comes to rest.)/Pg 53 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

1013 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 298 0 R/E(We often admire people who seem to know everything: what happens after death, whether God exists, or what the universe means. But try a more difficult exercise: take one belief you are certain of and ask, \215How do I know this?\216)/Pg 53 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

1014 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 298 0 R/E(This question is ancient. The Nasadiya Sukta of the Rigveda wonders about the origin of existence and ends by asking whether even the highest overseer knows its beginning. Rather than offering a final answer, the hymn leaves the question open.)/Pg 53 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

1015 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 298 0 R/E(Centuries later, the English biologist T. H. Huxley coined the word agnostic in 1869 for someone who does not claim knowledge of what cannot be established. In everyday language, agnosticism usually means uncertainty about God's existence.)/Pg 53 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

1016 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 298 0 R/T(Agnosticism: The Wisdom of Not Knowing)/E(Agnosticism: The Wisdom of Not Knowing)/Pg 53 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

1017 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 298 0 R/E(Compiled: Pragyadeep)/Pg 53 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

1018 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 298 0 R/E(Here, however, we explore a deeper meaning: not merely the absence of an answer, but the point where sincere enquiry has exposed the limits of knowledge and the need to possess an answer begins to fall away.)/Pg 53 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

1019 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 298 0 R/T(1. Knowing by Removing)/E(1. Knowing by Removing)/Pg 53 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

1020 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 298 0 R/E(We often think learning means collecting more facts, theories and beliefs. Imagine instead a dusty mirror. To see clearly, you do not add anything to it; you wipe away what obscures it.)/Pg 53 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

1021 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 298 0 R/E(The mind also carries layers of assumptions inherited from family, culture, religion and education. Some beliefs comfort us; others give us certainty or identity. But comfort does not establish truth. Believing that a higher power controls everything and believing that science will eventually explain everything are both claims that deserve examination.)/Pg 53 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

1022 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 298 0 R/E(In this sense, knowing becomes an act of removal.)/Pg 53 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

1023 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 298 0 R/E<FEFF092E09410915094D0924093F092C094B0927002D002000340032>/Pg 53 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

1024 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 298 0 R/Pg 53 0 R/A[2172 0 R 0]/K[12]>>
endobj

1025 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 298 0 R/E(AGnosticism:)/Pg 53 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

1026 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 298 0 R/E(The Wisdom of Not Knowing)/Pg 53 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

1027 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 298 0 R/E(Not a verdict about God, but the place)/Pg 53 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

1028 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 298 0 R/E(where knowing, having dropped everything false, comes to rest.)/Pg 53 0 R/K[16]>>
endobj

1029 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 299 0 R/E(Each false assumption that falls away allows greater clarity, without requiring a new belief to replace it.)/Pg 54 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

1030 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 299 0 R/E(The Upanishadic method of neti neti\204\215not this, not this\216\204points towards such enquiry. Whatever can be recognised as an assumption, an incomplete description or an object of thought is examined rather than accepted as the ultimate truth. The process continues until even the desire to define and possess truth comes under scrutiny.)/Pg 54 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

1031 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 299 0 R/E(This does not mean rejecting knowledge. It means using knowledge to examine what we believe, rather than turning it into something to defend.)/Pg 54 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

1032 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 299 0 R/T(2. The Limits of Knowledge)/E(2. The Limits of Knowledge)/Pg 54 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

1033 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 299 0 R/E(Consider a dream. Within it, your home, relationships and identity may seem completely real. Only on waking do you recognise that your understanding belonged to a particular state of experience. Our ordinary knowledge, too, depends on experience, interpretation and changing conditions.)/Pg 54 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

1034 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 299 0 R/E(A map can guide us, but it is not the landscape. In the same way, knowledge can describe, compare and interpret what appears, yet whether it can fully grasp the ultimate ground of existence remains a different question.)/Pg 54 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

1035 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 299 0 R/E(The intellect is valuable. It helps us examine assumptions, distinguish evidence from belief and recognise contradictions.)/Pg 54 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

1036 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 299 0 R/E(But its own conclusions also require examination. It cannot simply declare itself to have understood everything.)/Pg 54 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

1037 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 299 0 R/E(The Kena Upanishad expresses this difficulty through a paradox: what is understood by those who do not claim to have fully grasped it may be misunderstood by those who believe they have. The point is not that knowledge is useless, but that claiming complete knowledge may conceal the very limits we need to see.)/Pg 54 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

1038 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 299 0 R/E(A Zen story illustrates the same principle. Nan-in, a master, served tea to a professor and kept pouring until the cup overflowed. When the professor protested, Nan-in pointed out that a mind already full of opinions has little room to see anything new.)/Pg 54 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

1039 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 299 0 R/E(We can begin by asking three questions of a strongly held belief: How do I know it? By what means? What assumptions does it depend on?)/Pg 54 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

1040 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 299 0 R/E(These questions are instruments of enquiry, not a ritual to repeat forever. Their purpose is to help us see what can be established, what remains uncertain and where the intellect reaches its limits. When that purpose has been fulfilled, even the need to keep questioning can fall away.)/Pg 54 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

1041 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 299 0 R/T(Unknowability is not simply an intellectual conclusion. It is a recognition that no further conceptual answer may settle the deepest question of existence. The intellect has not failed; it has helped reveal where its own )/E(Unknowability is not simply an intellectual conclusion. It is a recognition that no further conceptual answer may settle the deepest question of existence. The intellect has not failed; it has helped reveal where its own )/Pg 54 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

1042 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 299 0 R/E<FEFF092E09410915094D0924093F092C094B0927002D002000340033>/Pg 54 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

1043 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 300 0 R/Pg 55 0 R/A[2170 0 R 0]/K[0]>>
endobj

1044 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 300 0 R/E(explanations cannot provide final certainty.)/Pg 55 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

1045 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 300 0 R/T(3. Who Is the Knower?)/E(3. Who Is the Knower?)/Pg 55 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

1046 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 300 0 R/E(Look at your hand, the room around you or your reflection in a mirror. These are things you can observe. Now turn your attention towards the act of observing itself.)/Pg 55 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

1047 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 300 0 R/E(Thoughts, feelings, sensations and even the sense of \215I\216 arise and are noticed. Can the one who knows them be found in the same way as another object?)/Pg 55 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

1048 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 300 0 R/E(This is an invitation to investigate, not a claim to accept on trust. The word witness can point towards this enquiry, but it should not become another fixed belief. If \215I am the witness\216 turns into a new identity, it too must be examined.)/Pg 55 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

1049 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 300 0 R/E(The question therefore shifts from \215What is true?\216 to \215Who is asking?\216 It is not intended to establish a new doctrine or identity. It asks us to examine the assumptions hidden in our very claim to know.)/Pg 55 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

1050 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 300 0 R/E(In the knowledge path described here, even a conceptual answer about the knower must eventually be released. Otherwise, the mind may simply replace one possession with another: an old belief gives way to a spiritual belief, and the search continues under a new name.)/Pg 55 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

1051 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 300 0 R/T(4. The Wisdom of Not Knowing)/E(4. The Wisdom of Not Knowing)/Pg 55 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

1052 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 300 0 R/E<>/Pg 55 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

1053 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 300 0 R/E(The Buddha responds with the story of a man struck by a poisoned arrow who refuses treatment until he knows every detail about the person who shot it.)/Pg 55 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

1054 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 300 0 R/E(The man risks losing his life while waiting for answers that do not address his immediate need. The Buddha directs attention towards suffering, its origin, its cessation and the path leading to its cessation.)/Pg 55 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

1055 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 300 0 R/E(His silence was a matter of priority, not a declaration that nothing can be known. The lesson is to distinguish questions that help end suffering and ignorance from questions that merely keep the mind occupied.)/Pg 55 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

1056 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 300 0 R/E(The Vedic poet, the sages of the Upanishads and the Buddha spoke in different contexts. They should not be treated as teaching one identical doctrine. Yet their distinct approaches invite us to examine how far our claims to knowledge can take us.)/Pg 55 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

1057 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 300 0 R/E(Not-knowing becomes wisdom only when it follows honest enquiry. Saying )/Pg 55 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

1058 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 300 0 R/E<FEFF092E09410915094D0924093F092C094B0927002D002000340034>/Pg 55 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

1059 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 301 0 R/E(\215I don't know\216 without examining anything may simply avoid the work. Recognising the limits of one's conclusions after careful examination is different. Agnosticism in this deeper sense is neither doubting everything nor believing nothing. It is not an escape from enquiry. It is the recognition that examining beliefs can remove false assumptions without necessarily producing a final concept that captures existence completely.)/Pg 56 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

1060 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 301 0 R/E(Even knowledge gained through enquiry must not become a possession. Knowledge serves its purpose when it helps remove ignorance; attachment to that knowledge can itself become another obstacle.)/Pg 56 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

1061 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 301 0 R/T(5. When Enquiry Comes to Rest)/E(5. When Enquiry Comes to Rest)/Pg 56 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

1062 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 301 0 R/E(Try a simple exercise. Choose one thing you are completely sure of. Ask how you know it, what supports it and which assumptions it depends on. Examine your answers without rushing to replace one belief with another.)/Pg 56 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

1063 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 301 0 R/E(Then turn towards the one who is asking. Notice the question, the desire for certainty and the urge to reach a conclusion. Do not force a special experience or accept an answer merely because it sounds profound.)/Pg 56 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

1064 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 301 0 R/E(This is a beginning, not a task that must continue forever. The aim is not to remain trapped in questions, but to see clearly enough that the need to collect, defend and possess answers can loosen.)/Pg 56 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

1065 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 301 0 R/E(In the sense explored here, agnosticism is more than a lack of knowledge. It is )/Pg 56 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

1066 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 301 0 R/E(what becomes possible when false assumptions have been examined and even attachment to knowing begins to fall away.)/Pg 56 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

1067 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 301 0 R/E(Nothing new needs to be established merely to replace what has been discarded. The enquiry comes to rest\204not in a final intellectual verdict, but in freedom from the need to possess one.)/Pg 56 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

